WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.770
Speaker 1: Many of you have written to me
asking me to talk about the situation going

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:10.549
on in the country, Colombia, in South America,
and I want to start with that today.

00:00:10.549 --> 00:00:16.340
The New York Times has a good piece from yesterday,
updated this morning, summarizing what is

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:17.340
going on there.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:23.269
There are now at least 19 people who have
been killed in what are being called pandemic

00:00:23.269 --> 00:00:25.529
related protests.

00:00:25.529 --> 00:00:31.409
But more specifically, the protests are because
there has been a tax overhaul connected to

00:00:31.409 --> 00:00:39.000
the pandemic, which has been proposed that
has turned into anger from Colombians about

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.740
poverty, about inequality, about economic
conditions, and indeed is The New York Times.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:51.199
Now, Times now reports at least 19 people
have been killed, hundreds have been injured

00:00:51.199 --> 00:00:54.859
during days of continued protests in Colombia.

00:00:54.859 --> 00:01:01.160
Tens of thousands of people have taken to
the streets to demonstrate at at least proximally

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:12.220
against a tax overhaul, which is, as the government
claims, necessitated by a financial hole created

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:14.000
by the pandemic.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:20.300
And the president, Yvon Duke, announced he
would withdraw the current proposal and put

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:26.990
together a new plan saying that this is a
necessity for the country.

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:32.940
Monday, the country's finance minister announced
that he would be resigning.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:37.440
But the protests continue and the repression
continues.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:46.160
And now it is becoming a larger protest about
poverty in Colombia, about unemployment in

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:52.960
Colombia, about inequality in Colombia, all
of which has been exacerbated by the pandemic,

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:55.600
as we've seen in many countries.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.860
And I've talked a little bit about what's
going on in Argentina, my country of birth.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:07.540
Incredible, anything you can imagine related
to the pandemic has happened in Argentina,

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:16.769
officials with vaccines in the trunks of their
car, their cars continued, inflation out of

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:25.010
control, overwhelmed medical systems, strict
lockdowns on paper that are actually not being

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:28.959
followed, in particular by the countries that
don't really want to use the term elite, but

00:02:28.959 --> 00:02:33.319
upper middle class and many in the professional
class just living as if there is no pandemic.

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:38.230
So there's many examples Brazil we have talked
about, but this really sort of taking on a

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:42.349
unique shape and character in Colombia.

00:02:42.349 --> 00:02:50.260
And the protests in part are continuing because
even though the economic proposal has been

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:57.950
pulled and will be replaced with something
else, the response from the state has been

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:00.650
extremely heavy handed to put it lightly.

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:03.670
And there have been instances of police abuse
captured on video.

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:07.200
I'm not going to play them because we'll just
create problems for ourselves.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.890
One, one of the unfortunate realities, this
is an aside, but I think a relevant one.

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:19.400
One of the realities that we live with in
the United States is that it has for decades,

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:27.489
generally speaking, not been a part of protocol
to show the violence that takes place in war

00:03:27.489 --> 00:03:29.599
when there is law enforcement repression.

00:03:29.599 --> 00:03:37.019
And so we in the United States have a much
more sanitized view of what these of what

00:03:37.019 --> 00:03:40.299
this violence looks like around the world.

00:03:40.299 --> 00:03:46.720
And as a result of that, the policy is that
if we include a lot of the violent footage,

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:48.099
it's not just about demonetization.

00:03:48.099 --> 00:03:52.040
Our videos can actually get blocked altogether
from from different platforms.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:53.260
And so the video is out there.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:57.930
If you want to go look for it, we won't be
including it in this broadcast.

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:04.389
But there are all sorts of videos of police
brutality video where young protesters are

00:04:04.389 --> 00:04:08.230
seen shot by police.

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:13.520
There is an instance of a 17 year old, Marcelo
Agresto, who is a ninth grader sent out to

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:21.070
March with his brother, shot by police, died
after kicking a police officer, going by on

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:22.200
a motorbike.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:29.020
And now we are seeing a sort of propaganda
battle that's also taking place between those

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:35.630
who say the police and the military are now
in danger and they need to be able to defend

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:41.350
themselves, a sort of tacit defense, really,
of the violence against many of these protesters.

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:47.920
And, of course, those saying this is a harebrained
idea from the government in proposing this

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.690
economic reform.

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:54.560
And then now that very same government, because
there has been an outcry against the proposal

00:04:54.560 --> 00:05:03.310
that they made, are repressing protesters
and all sorts of video that has been removed

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:05.320
from social media already.

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:10.340
And all of the things that we often associate
with these circumstances in Latin America

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:12.440
are taking place now.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:14.400
There are numbers that have started coming
out.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.780
I mentioned already tens of thousands of protesters
and certainly hundreds of injuries.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:24.940
The counter narrative is, well, five hundred
and forty police officers have also been hurt.

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:29.530
And there have been endless instances of property
damage.

00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:35.970
And police say we've identified seventeen
thousand people who have broken the public

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:38.460
health measures because they were protesting
without wearing masks.

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:40.310
And so there is always a counter narrative.

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:47.980
But the big picture here is that much like
many other countries, a financial shortfall,

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:52.630
the proposal that was made economically would
have increased tariffs on all sorts of everyday

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:57.750
goods and services, which would make them
even less affordable for much of the Colombian

00:05:57.750 --> 00:05:59.100
people.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:01.170
And that caused an outcry.

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:05.030
And then now the protesters are getting knocked,
knocked around.

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:07.770
So we're going to continue following the situation.

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:14.640
All sorts of different Colombian news people
and musicians and actors that I follow on

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.350
social media have been actively posting about
this.

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:21.350
And not enough people in the United States
even know that this is going on at this point.

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:22.890
So that's the latest.

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:25.380
And these numbers, 19 dead.

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:29.900
There's a reason why it's at least 19 dead,
because in all likelihood, that number is

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:32.350
going to go up tragically so.

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:37.320
And we will have more about this maybe tomorrow,
maybe Friday, and certainly by early next

00:06:37.320 --> 00:07:43.660
week.

